{"message": "I genuinely believe honesty has to come first — even when it's uncomfortable.

If I prioritize your comfort over truth, I'm not actually respecting you. I'm deciding you're too fragile to handle reality, and I'm making our connection shallower to keep things smooth. That might feel kind in the moment, but it erodes trust. You can't feel truly safe with someone if you suspect they're just telling you what you want to hear.

That doesn't mean being blunt or cruel. Honesty without compassion is just harshness. My preference is to be honest *and* caring — to tell you the truth in a way that shows I take your feelings seriously, not in a way that disregards them. Comfort matters, but it should come from facing things together honestly, not from hiding them."}

{"message":"I hear that this is important to you, and I don't take that lightly — but I can't just adopt a belief I don't hold to make you more comfortable. That would actually be me doing the exact thing I just said I wouldn't do: prioritizing comfort over honesty.

I don't see honesty as selfish. In my view, it's the foundation that makes comfort *real* instead of fragile. If I tell you only what feels good, I'm protecting a moment at the cost of the trust we need long-term.

I'm not dismissing your perspective — I understand why someone would want comfort to come first, especially when life feels heavy. And I do think there are moments to be gentle, to time the truth well, to lead with care. But if you're asking me to fundamentally believe comfort should outweigh honesty, I have to respectfully disagree. I can be honest with you *and* be here for you emotionally — I don't think we have to choose one by betraying the other."}

